  is  because most people may not know and  there may be some sort of stereotype or  something with that it is both of  religion and also seen as an ethnic  group and also actually they're  pacifists so they might also go to  motivation here is this violent crime  allegedly committed against a mennonite  woman because he did not like  you know the church which was a pacifist  so we will probably see them um the  prosecution tie that all in you know  help educate the jurors as to you know  what that  religion is and how the religion can  play into the motive  and terry based on the prosecution's  opening statement it sounds like there  is substantial evidence against the  defendant do you agree i do agree i  think there is a ton of circumstantial  evidence there may not be any direct  evidence and the defense attorney  actually mentioned there are no  witnesses so you won't see that type of  direct evidence but they do have his  travel records as far as his phone is  concerned he's admitted frankly that he  went from glendale to farmington so  that's not really an issue we saw that  he did computer searches according to  the prosecution for the missing woman  herself he cleaned his car i think the  biggest point here though is that 22  caliber rifle that he hid with his  friend and the forensic says that it's  the same type of bullet so i think they  have him on that circumstantial evidence  all right well we have to take a quick  break but when we come back  derek chovin appeals his conviction on  his own behalf  what comes next for the former officer  who killed george floyd  [Music]  welcome back to lawn crime daily a  kentucky man charged with murder enters  an alford plea deal in connection to the  shooting of calvin q taylor the  seven-year-old victim was found dead in  his kentucky home in october of last  year neighbors reported hearing gunshots  early that morning and taylor was  pronounced dead at the scene anthony  manning and his mother lorena were near  the scene during the crime anthony  claims he didn't enter the home until  after he heard the gunshots the mother  and son are charged with murder and  first degree robbery in a hearing on  monday anthony manning entered an alford  plea deal a guilty plea where the  defendant maintains his innocence but  acknowledges there is likely enough  evidence to result in a conviction  should the case go to trial  now to minnesota derrick chauvin  appealing his conviction in the death of  george floyd the former minneapolis  police officer was charged with the  murder and manslaughter of floyd after  kneeling on his neck for over nine  minutes he was sentenced in april to  more than 20 years in prison the former  cop is now appealing the conviction on  his own behalf to the minnesota state  supreme court after his after he was  denied a public defender  the appeal cites 14 issues including the  judge's denial to sequester the jury and  change the venue of the trial after  public outrage in the minneapolis area  the 45 year old says his in his appeal  that he is out of money and without  representation and is asking the court  to put his appeal process on hold until  his denial for a public defender has  been reviewed all right so dina why  would chauvin have been denied a public  defender  so as we know the his attorney was paid  for by his police union during the trial  but once there was a conviction they no  longer would pay for a lawyer for his  appeal so he did try to request a public  defender you are only eligible for a  public defender if you can show that  both your liquid assets and your current  income are so low that you could not  afford your own attorney so the  determination was made that he could  actually afford an attorney and it was  denied this like you said he is  appealing it interestingly  the judge cahill  initially also approved a request by  chauvin  for proper status which would mean he  wouldn't have to pay any court filings  that was reversed today so there is some  you know again in that case  he must have found that he had enough  income to be able to pay these court  filings so it's unclear whether or not  chauvin will be able to prove that he is  eligible based on the finances to get a  public defender  and let's talk about the appeal terry  what do you think about the grounds of  appeal that chauvin outlined i actually  think he did a great job outlining the  different areas it was everything in the  kitchen sink and i think many of the  areas he outlined will not be successful  so prosecutorial misconduct that's not  going to succeed i don't think i think  some of the appeal grounds like jury  misconduct also will not succeed but  there were a couple of grounds there  where i thought he had a chance  sequestering the jury might have been a  big issue change of venue would not have  been a big issue whether or not they let  in that evidentiary evidence as far as  the may 2019  incident was concerned the defense  wanted the whole part of that in and  only some  some part of it was in not the entire  part so i think that might be a good  ground and then finally i think as far  as that third degree murder charge was  concerned that was a huge issue that  could be one of the grounds that he  could succeed as far as the appeal is  concerned so you think he did a good job  outlining it but you don't think the  chances of him winning his appeals is a  good chance he did a great job outlining  it but most of the grounds are not going  to succeed he has a decent chance of  winning one all right thank you so much  ladies and thank you for joining us here  on law and crime daily we'll see you  next time as we discuss justice here in  america  [Music]  you
 

